WEBVTT

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:07.866
At picture size 1000, the 8-at-once method and the plain method each compute 1000

00:00:07.866 --> 00:00:08.840
values of cx.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:12.134
How many of the 1000 differ, even slightly?

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:13.952
Pause and write a number.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:32.534
The picture turns each place (i, j) into a middleman pair with two lines: cx =

00:00:32.534 --> 00:00:42.825
j/img_size × 2.5 − 2.0 and cy = i/img_size × 2.5 − 1.25, with img_size = 256.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:47.757
A CPU core normally does one operation on one number per instruction.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:55.272
The Ryzen in my laptop also has AVX2 instructions, which do the same operation on 8

00:00:55.272 --> 00:01:01.571
numbers at once, held side by side in a 256-bit register at index 0 to 7.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.409
The lab asks for the picture to be computed with them, by handling 8 neighbouring

00:01:06.409 --> 00:01:07.296
places together.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:12.578
The first wall is the input: each of the 8 places needs its own middleman pair,

00:01:12.578 --> 00:01:14.765
built before any arithmetic begins.

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:21.131
The vector code builds them with a line containing 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0, numbers

00:01:21.131 --> 00:01:23.696
that appear nowhere in the problem.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:28.339
Can those numbers be forced out of the original cx line, so they stop being

00:01:28.339 --> 00:01:29.597
something to remember?

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:36.911
And a harder question: the vector code matched the plain code exactly on the 256 by

00:01:36.911 --> 00:01:37.919
256 picture.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:40.403
Is that proof that the method is exact?

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:55.938
In English: offsets is a 256-bit variable holding 8 fractions. _mm256_set_ps fills

00:01:55.938 --> 00:02:03.611
them from index 7 down to index 0, so index k holds k. dx is 2.5 divided by

00:02:03.611 --> 00:02:04.531
img_size.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:11.141
For each i, compute one cy the plain way and copy it into all 8 indexes of cy_vec.

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:17.335
Then j takes 0, 8, 16 and so on, so each step covers 8 values of j.

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:24.572
For each j, compute one cx; then, at each of the 8 indexes, multiply offsets by dx

00:02:24.572 --> 00:02:30.203
and add cx, all in one fused multiply-add instruction, into cx_vec.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:37.926
The 8 indexes share one i, and cy reads only i, so all 8 need the same cy.

00:02:37.926 --> 00:02:43.009
_mm256_set1_ps copies one float into all 8 indexes.

00:02:43.360 --> 00:02:44.128
Pause.

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:50.112
For i = 40, what is cy, and how many times is cy computed for the whole picture?

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:10.396
Here 40/256 = 0.15625; times 2.5 that is 0.390625; minus 1.25 gives −0.859375,

00:03:10.396 --> 00:03:15.041
copied into all 8 indexes.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:21.615
It is computed once per i, 256 times, and it sits above the j loop because nothing

00:03:21.615 --> 00:03:23.817
in it changes when j changes.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:24.938
Pause.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.381
Fill indexes 1, 3 and 7.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:35.239
Then subtract index 0 from each, and divide each difference by 2.5/256.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:55.734
The cx values are 0.041015625, 0.060546875 and 0.099609375.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:09.371
The differences are 0.009765625, 0.029296875 and 0.068359375.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:21.901
Divided by 2.5/256 = 0.009765625, they give 1, 3 and 7: the index itself.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:37.727
So cx at index k = cx at index 0 + k × 0.009765625, and 0.009765625 is exactly dx.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:40.139
The numbers 0 to 7 are that k.

00:04:40.490 --> 00:04:46.143
The problem never typed them; 8 places sharing one j forces them out of the cx line.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:47.258
Pause.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:48.890
Fill the bottom line.

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:08.859
The bottom line reads 0.03125, 0.041015625, 0.05078125, and so on up to 0.099609375

00:05:08.859 --> 00:05:14.931
at index 7, the same as the long way.

00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:21.936
It is exact here because 256 is a power of 2, and my program reported 0 mismatches.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:30.055
_mm256_set_ps takes its first argument into index 7 and its last into index 0.

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:37.717
Suppose I had typed _mm256_set_ps(0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7).

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:38.838
Pause.

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:45.354
What does index 0 hold, which cx does the place j = 208 get, and are the counts

00:05:45.354 --> 00:05:47.382
wrong or merely misplaced?

00:05:53.730 --> 00:06:06.615
Index 0 holds 7, so j = 208 gets cx = 0.099609375, which is the cx of 215.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:09.295
Every block of 8 comes out mirror-reversed.

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:18.077
For i = 40 the picture stores 5 5 6 6 6 7 8 12 where it should store 12 8 7 6 6 6 5

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:18.280
5.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:21.318
Every count is right, but stored in the wrong place.

00:06:21.670 --> 00:06:25.873
A compare against the plain output catches it; looking at the picture may not.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:28.788
Pause.

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:30.783
Fill the counts and the percent.

00:06:31.130 --> 00:06:37.321
Then write one sentence on what the "0 mismatches" test at 256 proved, and one on

00:06:37.321 --> 00:06:39.002
what it did not prove.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:53.872
The plain code does 65,536 cx lines and 65,536 divides.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:07:02.689
The vector code does 8192 of each plus 8192 fmadd, so 8 times fewer divides.

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:07.456
At size 1000, 0.33 percent of the places change.

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:14.199
The test at 256 proved that the fmadd method gives exactly the plain values at that

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:14.584
size.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:21.722
It did not prove the method is exact in general: it was exact because 256 is a power

00:07:21.722 --> 00:07:25.436
of 2, so dx and every k × dx are exact floats.

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:31.013
The tempting answer, "0 mismatches, so the method is exact", fails because a test

00:07:31.013 --> 00:07:34.953
succeeds for a reason, and here the reason was the test size.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:42.358
Doing 8 things at once forces you to describe the 8 with one rule; here the rule is

00:07:42.358 --> 00:07:45.835
that neighbour k sits k steps of dx from the first one.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:50.340
Finding that rule is the actual work; typing the instruction is not.

00:07:50.690 --> 00:07:55.953
For a manager: a test that succeeds on round numbers can hide a defect that appears

00:07:55.953 --> 00:07:57.538
only on ordinary numbers.

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:00.066
Ask what the test size was, and why.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:09.345
The answer is 572 of the 1000 values of cx, computed in 32-bit fractions.

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:10.735
Mark your tally.

00:08:12.890 --> 00:08:17.503
How would you make the 8-at-once method give exactly the plain method's values at

00:08:17.503 --> 00:08:19.781
any picture size, and what does it cost?

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:20.898
Pause.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:32.863
Build the 8 values of j + k as fractions in one register, then apply the same

00:08:32.863 --> 00:08:38.258
divide, multiply and subtract as the plain line, at all 8 indexes at once.

00:08:38.600 --> 00:08:42.163
The rounding then happens in the same steps as the plain code.

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:47.754
The cost is an 8-at-once divide per block of 8, which is the very step the shortcut

00:08:47.754 --> 00:08:48.259
avoided.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:53.697
Also read the plain code's machine listing: the compiler may fuse its multiply and

00:08:53.697 --> 00:08:58.669
subtract into one multiply-add, and the 8-at-once version must then do the same.

